1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
In this lecture, we will cover floating point numbers.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
First, we'll learn about how floating point numbers are represented in binary.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Then we'll look at how to perform arithmetic with them.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
To start, we motivate why we need a floating point representation in the first place.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
In many applications, we want to be able to represent a large range of numbers without needing much precision.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:39,000
For example, if we wanted to represent numbers ranging from 3 to 1,572,864 in a typical integer or fixed point encoding,

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
we would need at least 21 bits.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The value 3 is all leading zeros followed by 1,1, while the value 1,572,864 is 1,1, followed by all trailing zeros.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
But these running zeros are a waste of space in the binary encoding.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The value 3 is just 1.1 times 2 to the first, while the big number is just 1.1 times 2 to the 20th.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
So for both numbers, we really only need two bits of information and an exponent value.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The exponent effectively tells us where the point is.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
For 3, the point is in bit position 1, while for the large number, the point is in bit position 20.

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
This notation is referred to as floating point.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Notice how it's very similar to the scientific notation that we use for very large or very small decimal numbers.

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The floating point format consists of three binary fields, a sign bit, an exponent field, and the fraction field.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Looking at the value formula, you can see that if the sign bit is 1, then the encoded value is negative.

18
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
The exponent bits specify at which bit position the point is located.

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
And the fraction bits specify the actual number that's encoded minus the implicit leading 1.

20
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Here, we're showing an example floating point representation that uses a 4-bit exponent and a 5-bit fraction.

21
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:18,000
As shown, the decimal number 9.25 can be represented as 1.00101 times 2 to the third in binary.

22
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Thus, the exponent value is 3 and the fraction is 00101.

23
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
There are two things to note.

24
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:33,000
First, the significant is generally normalized such that there is an implicit or hidden leading 1 in front of the point.

25
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,000
With that, the leading 1 doesn't have to be encoded and we can use that bit for exponent or fraction.

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The second thing is that the exponent is often stored in biased form.

27
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
We will discuss this more in the coming slides.

28
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The IEEE 754 standard defines two precision levels for floating point numbers.

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
In single 32-bit precision, the exponent is 8 bits and the fraction is 23 bits.

30
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Single precision floats can represent numbers roughly as high as 2 to the 127th and as low as 2 to the negative 126th.

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:19,000
In double or 64-bit precision, the exponent is 11 bits and the significant is 52 bits.

32
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
It can represent a broader range of numbers.

33
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Why is the significant normalized?

34
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Without normalization, we end up with multiple ways of representing the same number.

35
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:46,000
For example, the decimal number 9.25 can be represented as 1001.01 times 2 to the 0 or 100.101 times 2 to the 1st and so on.

36
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
So the standard encoding is to normalize this significant such that we only have one leading bit in front of the binary point.

37
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Since every number has this leading 1, this bit becomes implicit and is not encoded, leaving just the fraction field.

38
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Now this way, every number has a unique representation.

39
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
In floating point numbers, the exponent can either be positive or negative.

40
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The latter is used to represent small numbers that are close to zero.

41
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:26,000
As mentioned briefly earlier, it is generally preferred to encode the exponent in a biased or excess notation instead of in two's complement.

42
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The reason for this is to simplify sorting.

43
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
It's much easier to sort numbers if a larger exponent value implies a larger floating point value.

44
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Floating point numbers with negative exponents are smaller in value than numbers with positive exponents.

45
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:49,000
However, when viewed as unsigned values, negative exponents seem bigger since their most significant bits are one.

46
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
So instead, exponents are generally encoded based on a predefined bias value.

47
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Revisiting our example from earlier, we can use a biased notation for the exponent where the predefined bias is seven.

48
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:14,000
So now if we wish to represent two to the third, for example, we actually set E to be ten instead of three since ten minus seven equals three.

49
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:23,000
IEEE 754 uses a bias of 127 for single precision floating point and a bias of 1,023 for double precision.

50
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
This table shows examples how exponent values are encoded in single precision.

51
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Notice how with two's complement exponents, it becomes difficult to sort values.

52
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
You would have to do a signed comparison of the exponent and then perform an unsigned comparison of the six significance just to compare two floating point numbers.

53
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:50,000
On the other hand, by using a bias, larger exponent values always have a larger unsigned encoding as shown in the rightmost column.

54
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Thus comparing two floating point numbers becomes as simple as unsigned integer comparison.

55
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Note that the IEEE floating point standard also supports special cases shown here.

56
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
First, the value zero is simply encoded as all zeros in both single and double precision.

57
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:18,000
For numbers that are very small and close to zero, we don't actually normalize the significant anymore.

58
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
These cases are specified by an exponent value of zero, which is the smallest exponent supported.

59
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Conversely, infinity can be encoded by setting the maximum exponent and setting the significant bits to zero.

60
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:40,000
And lastly, undefined values, or not a number, are encoded by setting the max exponent with a non-zero significant.

61
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Note that denormalized numbers allow for gradualized underflow. You get smaller numbers and you do not quite underflow quite as quickly.

62
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Here's a website where you can look at setting your own values.

63
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Next, we'll discuss floating point overflow and underflow.

64
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Floating point overflow is analogous to integer overflow in that the result is too large to be represented.

65
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
This is detected when the exponent is too big.

66
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Floating point underflow is the reverse when the result is too small or too negative.

67
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Both of these scenarios can trigger an exception under the IEEE standard, if enabled.

68
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Now we'll move on to how to perform addition on floating point numbers.

69
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
It turns out that this is just like adding decimal numbers in scientific notation, as shown in the example here.

70
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Given two numbers, first thing you have to do is align their points on top of each other by shifting the smaller number.

71
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Then from there, we can directly add the significant values as in regular integer addition.

72
00:07:48,000 --> 00:08:00,000
And then finally, we normalize the sum so that there's only one digit in front of the decimal point and then round the final sum.

73
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Now we'll apply this process to the binary floating point representation from earlier.

74
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Now we're adding two values, 1.11001 times 2 to the third and 1.1001 times 2 to the first.

75
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
For reference, the exponents are shown here in biased form as well.

76
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:27,000
So first thing we do is shift the second operand to the right since it's smaller so that we can align the points.

77
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Now that both are normalized to 2 to the third, we can directly add them using integer arithmetic.

78
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:40,000
After adding, we find that the sum has two leading bits so we need to perform normalization.

79
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
Thus, we shift the sum to the right by one, which consequently increases the exponent by one.

80
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:58,000
Then finally, since the significant needs to be six bits only, we round the value and drop the least significant bits.

81
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
This slide illustrates the typical hardware organization of a floating point adder.

82
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,000
From top to bottom, first we need to compare the exponents of the incoming operands using a small ALU

83
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:14,000
so that we can identify the smaller operand and shift its significant to line up the points.

84
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Then we need a bigger ALU to add the aligned significance.

85
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Then, depending on the resulting sum, we need to shift the significant left or right to normalize it,

86
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,000
which consequently increments or decrements the exponent value.

87
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Then finally, we round the sum.

88
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Let's dig a little deeper into rounding.

89
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Turns out, rounding is surprisingly very important because with the billions of operations that a typical program performs,

90
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:49,000
small rounding errors can really accumulate and have a major impact on the program's result.

91
00:09:49,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Let's go through the previous example once again, except now, we'll account for the fact that our hardware would only have a six-bit adder since the significant is only six bits.

92
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,000
Because of this, some least significant bits are dropped during the sum computation.

93
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Then, with normalization, we may end up dropping even more bits.

94
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:19,000
And because all of these lost bits, we're unable to make an accurate decision of whether to round up or round down.

95
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
To support rounding for addition, the FP adder actually needs to introduce a guard bit.

96
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:29,000
The guard bit is effectively just an extra bit that we append to the right of the significant.

97
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:36,000
This allows us to retain an extra bit of precision in the values, and thus we can use a seven-bit adder.

98
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Now, even after normalization, the guard bit is able to retain an extra bit, which happens to be one in this case.

99
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Now with this, we're able to make a better decision to either round up or round down appropriately.

100
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
Now we'll move on to floating point multiplication.

101
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
To perform multiplication, we need to do a few things.

102
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
First, we need to compute the signed bit of the product.

103
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
This is simply a matter of XORing the signed bits of the operands.

104
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Second, we need to compute the product's exponent.

105
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
To do this, we add the biased exponent values of the operands.

106
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Since the two input exponent values are already biased, the sum will be doubly biased, so we need to subtract the bias once.

107
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Then, to compute the product's significant, we simply perform standard integer multiplication on the significant as they are.

108
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Note that no shifting nor alignment needs to be performed before multiplying them.

109
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Then, after this, we may need to re-normalize and round, just like in addition, where we shift the significant in increment or decrement exponent accordingly.

110
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Here we outline the specific steps that need to be taken.

111
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:56,000
First, we compute the sign, exponent, and significance of the product, as just discussed.

112
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Then we perform normalization incrementally.

113
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Depending on the current significant value, we shift left or right once.

114
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Then we check for overflow or underflow and round the significant.

115
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Then, we check again, and if it's still not normalized, go back to step two.

116
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Here we show an example of floating point multiplication.

117
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
We'll assume we have a guard bit for rounding, as introduced earlier.

118
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Let's start with computing the exponent.

119
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:34,000
We need to add A's biased exponent, which is 10, with B's biased exponent, which is 8.

120
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:44,000
This gives us 18, but then we need to subtract the bias of 7, so the product's biased exponent value is 11, which represents 2 to the 4th.

121
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Now let's compute the significant hand by simply performing fixed point multiplication on A and B's significant hands.

122
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
Note that this yields a 6-bit product with an extra guard bit.

123
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Now, we normalize by shifting the significant right by 1 bit and incrementing the exponent.

124
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:10,000
And then finally, using the guard bit, we round the final 6-bit product.

125
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Let's look at another example with different values for A and B.

126
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:19,000
As before, we compute the product's exponent and significant hand values.

127
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:26,000
But this time, note that the leading bit of the significant hand is 0 after multiplying the input's significant hands.

128
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:34,000
As a result, after we normalize, we're actually left with a guard bit as among the 6-bits of the significant hand.

129
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:45,000
As a result, when we get to rounding, we don't have any bits left beyond the guard bit, and this leads to a potential rounding error.

130
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
So with this, let's revisit floating point rounding hardware.

131
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Modern implementations usually introduce two more bits in addition to the guard bit.

132
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:59,000
The first is a round bit, which just serves as an extra guard bit for floating point multiplication.

133
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
As shown in the previous example, sometimes multiplying the significant hands results in a leading 0-bit.

134
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:10,000
This leads to the guard bit getting shifted into the significant hand itself.

135
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
So the round bit allows us to retain an additional bit for rounding in this particular case.

136
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:21,000
The second additional bit is the sticky bit, which effectively serves as the final guard bit.

137
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:26,000
During the multiplication and normalization, several bits may get shifted out and dropped.

138
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:35,000
The sticky bit gets set to 1 if any of those drop bits are 1, so it's effectively a logical oar of all of these bits.

139
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:45,000
This allows us to remember if there were any 1 bits that we dropped in order to help us decide when appropriate to round up.

140
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Now with this, let's redo the previous example.

141
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:56,000
This time, when we compute the product's significant hand, we retain not only a guard bit, but also a round bit and a sticky bit,

142
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
where the sticky bit is the logical oar of all the other bits that were dropped.

143
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:07,000
As before, the guard bit ends up in the 6-bit significant hand, but now that we've retained additional bits,

144
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:13,000
we have more information to accurately round the final product.

145
